===Adjective===
{{en-adj|thinner}}
[[File:21 inch waist.jpg|thumb|Woman with a '''thin''' waist]]

# Having little [[thickness]] or [[extent]] from one [[surface]] to its opposite.
#: {{ux|en|'''thin''' plate of metal;&nbsp; '''thin''' paper;&nbsp; '''thin''' board;&nbsp; '''thin''' covering}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|7
|passage=Out of spite, the human beings pretended not to believe that it was Snowball who had destroyed the windmill: they said that it had fallen down because the walls were too '''thin'''.}}
# Very [[narrow]] in all diameters; having a [[cross section]] that is small in all directions.
#: {{ux|en|'''thin''' wire; '''thin''' string}}
# Having little body fat or flesh; [[slim]]; [[slender]]; [[lean]]; [[gaunt]].
#: {{ux|en|'''thin''' person}}
# Of low [[viscosity]] or low [[specific gravity]].
#: {{ux|en|Water is '''thinner''' than honey.}}
# [[scarce|Scarce]]; not close, crowded, or numerous; not filling the space.
#: {{ux|en|The trees of a forest are '''thin'''; the corn or grass is '''thin'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Italy
|passage=Ferrara is very large, but extremely '''thin''' of people.}}
# {{lb|en|golf}} Describing a poorly played [[golf]] shot where the ball is struck by the bottom part of the club head. See [[fat]], [[shank]], [[toe]].
# Lacking body or volume; small; feeble; not full.
#: {{ux|en|a '''thin''', tight-lipped smile}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Don Sebastian
|passage='''thin''', hollow sounds, and lamentable screams}}
# Slight; small; slender; flimsy; superficial; inadequate; not sufficient for a covering.
#: {{ux|en|a '''thin''' disguise}}
# {{lb|en|aviation}} Of a route: relatively little used.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2016|author=Hartmut Wolf; Peter Forsyth; David Gillen|title=Liberalization in Aviation|page=105
|passage=In short, we previously found that '''thin''' routes benefit from an increase in competition in the Spanish airline market when considering routes that were monopoly routes in 2001.}}
# Poor; scanty; without money or success.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1945|author=Jack Henry|title=What Price Crime?|page=92
|passage=Like their friends the "draggers," the "hoisters" or shoplifters are having a '''thin''' time these days, {{...}}}}

